    note: "⛔ BOTH CAPABILITIES WERE ALREADY IN THE REPO WITH NO READER, WHICH IS THIS SESSION'S RECURRING CLASS ARRIVING AS A WHOLE FEATURE. scripts/world/terrain.mjs has carried a view-culled generator since SNG-391 — its own header says 'only the parameters that can affect the current window are considered, which is what makes a regional zoom affordable' — and the globe never called it. The asset has carried 113 traced rivers, 17 lakes and 38 marshes since the same ticket, re-anchored by polar signature across SNG-393/394 through two collision censuses, and `grep hydrology engine/worldglobe.js app.js` returned ZERO. Erik found both by opening Aevi's prototype and noticing it could do things the shipped game could not. ⚠️ MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: one raster cell is ~11 screen pixels at the OLD zoom ceiling — the staircase coastlines in his screenshot — and the generator resolves 0.02° apart as different ground, 37× finer than a cell. Driven live in the browser: 1035 of 1036 sampled pixels at a 13° span match the generator rendering and ZERO match the raster. ⛔ THE SEAM IS THE REAL ENGINEERING RISK AND IT ALREADY HAPPENED ONCE — rebuild.py's header lists 'the base globe and the detail patch drawing different worlds (a visible seam)' among the regressions that forced the pipeline to exist. Two gates hold it shut, and the second one FAILED FIRST and was right to: the shipped DEM is HYDROLOGICALLY ADJUSTED (authored digs, three smoothing passes, pit fill) while the generator returns the surface before any of that — median difference 1, p99 28, max 90 of a 126-unit range, worst exactly where water was dug, which is where a reader looks. So the generator does not REPLACE elevation, it adds only the variation WITHIN a cell: at a cell centre the correction is zero by construction and the seam cannot open, between centres 55.7% of land carries real sub-cell relief up to 54.9 units. ⚠️ AND THE FIRST FORM OF THAT ADDITION WAS SILENTLY ZERO — I differenced two ALREADY-ROUNDED elevations, so sub-unit relief quantised away and the feature would have shipped as an expensive no-op; the 'must add something' half of the gate caught it, and the delta is taken in continuous space now, rounded once at the end. ⛔ THEN I SHIPPED IT AND ERIK SAID \"zooming seems to crash it\" — AND IT WAS NOT A CRASH, WHICH IS WORSE. The first form called the generator once per screen pixel plus four more for the hillshade gradient: 378,000 pixels × 5 × 6µs = ELEVEN SECONDS of blocked main thread per repaint, measured. ⚠️ MY OWN VERIFICATION HAD MISSED IT IN THE MOST INSTRUCTIVE WAY: I waited 2500ms, screenshotted, and read a HALF-PAINTED canvas as success — the painted half was genuinely correct, which is exactly why it convinced me. A timing bug looks like a passing test if you only check what rendered. The fix is the shape Aevi's prototype already had and I had read without understanding: sample the generator onto a small BUFFER over the visible window, sized from a MEASURED per-call cost against a millisecond budget, then interpolate per pixel — 11.21s → 0.30s, with the gesture itself staying coarse and sharpening 140ms after it settles. ⛔ AND THE FIX CARRIED TWO DEFECTS OF ITS OWN, BOTH FOUND BY LOOKING AGAIN RATHER THAN BY A GATE. First, cold calibration: the first calls run interpreted and timed 37.5µs against a warm 6µs, six times pessimistic, pinning the buffer to its floor — a performance guard that silently disables the feature it guards is worse than none, so the probe warms up first. Second, and this one made the feature actively HARMFUL: the buffer was SQUARE while the window is padded by 1/cos(lat), so near the Crossing it spanned 24° of latitude against SEVENTY-ONE of longitude and sampled 0.98° east-west against the raster's 0.75° — the detail path drew a COARSER world more slowly and nothing said so. The buffer matches the window's ground shape now, and `worthIt` makes a patch that cannot beat the bake DECLINE instead of degrading. ⛔ THE LAST STEP WAS FREE AND MATTERED MOST: the coastline was still a staircase because land/sea came from the NEAREST sample. sign(raw) IS the shoreline — 100.000% against the generator over 20,000 samples — so the interpolated field crosses zero exactly where the coast runs, and reading the crossing gives a shore finer than the samples that drew it. Measured on the live canvas: longest flat run along the boundary 32px (raster) → 14px (square buffer) → 9px, mean 1.89.",
